## PASO 2 — IMPLEMENTAR SCALER

---

Usar:

```
sklearn.preprocessing.StandardScaler
```

Inicializar:

```
scaler = StandardScaler()
```

Entrenarlo exclusivamente:

```
scaler.fit(X_train)
```

Transformar:

```
X_train_scaled = scaler.transform(X_train)
X_val_scaled   = scaler.transform(X_validation)
X_test_scaled  = scaler.transform(X_test)
```

Después construir las secuencias.

IMPORTANTE:

El scaler debe aplicarse antes de construir las ventanas,
manteniendo exactamente el mismo sequence_length.

==================================================

## PASO 3 — EVITAR CAMBIO DE MUESTRAS

---

El scaling NO debe alterar:

```
número de samples
timestamps
labels
sequence_length
```

Para:

```
sequence_length = 30
```

debe mantenerse:

```
TRAIN sequences = n_train - 29
```

y el mecanismo de contexto temporal debe permanecer exactamente
igual.

==================================================

## PASO 4 — BASELINE CONTROLADO

---

Ejecutar primero el LSTM actual SIN scaling.

Registrar:

```
epochs
sequence_length
seed
architecture
optimizer
learning_rate
batch_size
```

y:

```
validation ROC-AUC
validation PR-AUC
validation F1

test ROC-AUC
test PR-AUC
test F1
```

Este será el baseline.

==================================================

## PASO 5 — EXPERIMENTO SCALED

---

Entrenar el mismo LSTM con:

```
StandardScaler
```

Mantener EXACTAMENTE iguales:

```
arquitectura
epochs
sequence_length
optimizer
learning rate
batch size
labels
threshold
train/validation/test split
```

La única diferencia debe ser:

```
feature scaling
```

==================================================

